+| `getCapacityScenario` GET `/capacity-plans/{capacityPlanId}/scenarios/{scenarioId}` | 200; 404 `UNKNOWN_CAPACITY_SCENARIO` | common 400/401/403/503 | Same plan/scoped scenario only; foreign scope 404. No replay. |
+| `evaluateCapacityScenario` POST `/capacity-plans/{capacityPlanId}/scenarios/{scenarioId}/evaluations` | 202 Accepted; 404 `UNKNOWN_CAPACITY_SCENARIO`; 409 `EVALUATION_ALREADY_ACTIVE` | 409 `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED`; **422 `INVALID_CONSTRAINT_REFERENCE`** for resourceRefs absent from or incompatible with the exact scoped fixture; common 400/401/403/503 | Different key while Accepted/Running → declared 409; same key/body original 202, no second job. Invalid fixture resourceRefs → 422 with zero evaluation/receipt/audit/outbox. Terminal fixture-only Completed/Failed is not a general optimizer claim. |
+| `getCapacityEvaluation` GET `/capacity-plans/{capacityPlanId}/evaluations/{evaluationId}` | 200; 404 `UNKNOWN_CAPACITY_EVALUATION` | common 400/401/403/503 | Read Accepted/Running/Completed/Failed without mutation; foreign scope 404. No replay. |
+
+## Successor-only createCapacityScenario duplicate exact-name disposition
+
+Only POST `/capacity-plans/{capacityPlanId}/scenarios` changes. An active (`IsDeleted=false`) scenario with the same exact parsed `name` in the same authenticated TenantId, LegalEntityId and CapacityPlanId conflicts under simple binary collation. No trim, case folding, Unicode normalization or new maximum length is introduced. Different case, leading/trailing whitespace, Unicode sequences, plan or authorized tenant/LE scope do not themselves collide; a deleted scenario does not occupy the active-name uniqueness key. Alternate JSON escape spelling of the same decoded name is still the same name. This does not grant cross-scope target access or bypass existing schema/fixture validation.
+
+For a valid request with no replay, an eligible Draft parent and valid existing constraint references, deterministic duplicate detection and a **proven unique scenario-name index collision** both return HTTP 409 `CAPACITY_SCENARIO_NAME_CONFLICT`, message `Capacity scenario name already exists in this plan`. They use the unchanged Error schema and wire `contractVersion: v1`; `X-Correlation-Id` and `error.correlationId` both carry the current valid request UUID. Existing 409 codes/examples remain valid; the new code is not an alias for `CAPACITY_PLAN_STATE_CONFLICT` or a new shared error policy.
+
+Preserve the existing order: authentication/scope/schema/target checks precede receipt lookup; identical valid same-key request replays its original 201/body before current lifecycle, fixture or name checks; changed valid payload with that key returns `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED`. Without a matching receipt, non-Draft parent returns `CAPACITY_PLAN_STATE_CONFLICT` before name collision. Existing constraint validation stays before the name check: an invalid constraint tuple returns the existing 422, not a name conflict. No lifecycle transition is added.
+
+For two otherwise valid different-key requests racing for the same active exact name in one eligible scope, one committed create returns 201 and the losing unique-name operation returns the same defined 409 as deterministic detection, with zero additional scenario, receipt, audit or Pending outbox for the loser. Roll back any staged losing write set. Resolve a same-key receipt before classifying a duplicate-key error: a receipt/index collision is not automatically a scenario-name collision. Revalidation must preserve target, receipt, lifecycle and fixture precedence. An arbitrary duplicate-key error, exhausted transient retries or unresolved commit is not proof of a name conflict. Existing known-failure 503 and receipt-first unknown-commit recovery remain unchanged: unresolved commit returns `COMMIT_RESULT_UNRESOLVED`, with no zero-write claim; a found receipt returns its original result. No speculative 409 may mask uncertain commit or reconciliation read failure.
+
+These are contract acceptance requirements, not evidence that the production unique-index race implementation is fixed. Source application requires this contract to be separately approved and published, separate runtime authorization, and the consumer/cutover decision.
+
+## Lifecycle/fixture boundary that published wire errors depend on
+
+0190: Create Draft/null; capture from Draft or InReview appends immutable snapshot and sets InReview; sign-off only InReview against any same-plan snapshot, one decision per snapshot/role, no automatic plan Approved/Rejected even after all five roles approve. The sign-off state-conflict code is included in the published 2.0.0 contract. 0192: Capacity plan/scenario remain Draft in this bounded slice; evaluation alone may progress Accepted→Running→Completed/Failed under the **design-approved** module-owned executor, with runtime authorization recorded separately. The owner approved the startup+10-second scan, 30-second fenced lease and three-attempt **design for candidate preparation**, including the exact `DECISION.md` SHA-256 above; this contract does not itself grant runtime implementation authority. Terminal values come only from the exact fixture oracle `CAPACITY-EVAL-FIXTURE-192-01@1`; no live optimizer, supply-constraint source or Event Bus publisher. A Pending outbox event is written at terminal commit, no publisher delivery claim. Terminal E4 acceptance requires separately recorded Phase 1.5 and runtime DEV/VER authorization and evidence.
+
+The four approved scope limits are respected: exact tenant/LE test fixture for DEMAND with no producer endpoint; local trusted JWT actor/no Workflow; atomic Pending outbox/no publisher; Capacity fixture references/literal results only. A fixture PASS is not live DEMAND version/checksum or constraint producer validation. The 400/409/422/503 wire cases are included in the published amendment. Earlier executor/lifecycle/replay approvals were for candidate preparation; exact-hash consumer consent and publication were granted separately. Static contract results alone do not prove runtime uptake.
+
+
+## MOD-0192 executor design bound to exact approved decision hash
+
+The controlling design source is `docs/roadmap/plans/mod-0192-executor-exact-decisions-01/DECISION.md` SHA-256 `cfddf953a1a98107869417ef8afbcad5f578fcfde2a8cad8e1d2b6b05197cc5d`. The historical document itself remains unchanged and says PROPOSED; the later user message approved **that exact payload as design during candidate preparation**; subsequent exact-hash publication did not authorize runtime implementation.
+
+- A successful scoped claim of Accepted or expired Running atomically writes `status=Running`, version, a new monotonic fence, `leaseUntil`, and increments persisted `attempt` **in that claim**. Initial attempt is 0; successful claims are 1, 2, 3. Losing CAS is not an attempt; a claim followed by crash/expiry is.
+- Claim, renewal, expiry and terminal validity use Mongo primary **server UTC time**, not process/JWT/request time. A lease expires at `leaseUntil <= serverNow`. Each successful claim/renewal gives `serverNow + 30s`; target Mongo support for server-side time expressions is a Phase 1.5 technical gate, not assumed.
+- The executor signals after committed Accepted and scans on startup and every 10 seconds. While Running, a separate 10-second heartbeat renews only under current scope/status/version/fence and unexpired lease. Renewal does not increment attempt. A worker losing renewal does not attempt terminal persistence; any stale result is rejected by the fence.
+- Terminal commit requires scoped `Running`, current version/fence and `serverNow < leaseUntil`. A losing CAS writes no aggregate, slot, audit or outbox. Winning transaction atomically writes terminal Completed/Failed with `completedAt`, releases the active slot, and writes one terminal audit and one `capacity.evaluation.completed.v1` **Pending** outbox row. Event `occurredAt=completedAt`, original submit correlation and scenario-created causation. A unique terminal effect key prevents a second terminal event. Unknown terminal commit is resolved by reading scoped evaluation/event/audit before retry, never by blind second insert.
+- A third successful claim may complete normally while its lease is valid. If claim 3 crashes/expires, startup/scan first resolves any uncertain terminal commit, then performs a fenced atomic Failed terminal transaction **without claim 4 or fixture invocation 4**. Deterministic fixture rejection uses the same Failed path. Literal fixture oracle may run more than once after crashes; the guarantee is one durable terminal effect, not exactly-once computation.
+- Publisher remains absent; terminal outbox stays Pending. The executor is module-owned evaluation progress, not Event Bus delivery. Ten-second intervals and 30-second lease are configured intervals, not recovery SLA guarantees.
+
+The X01–X10 interruption/restart oracles in the approved `DECISION.md` are acceptance requirements for a later authorized isolated runtime DEV/VER; this static annex cannot establish those E4 outcomes.
+
+## Acceptance controls for independent VER
+
+1. Verify baseline and published target hashes, exact patch application and only this YAML/annex scope; compare operationId/path/security/body schemas/event schemas to baseline. No DEMAND YAML edit.
+2. Validate OpenAPI 3.1 schema, local refs and every positive/negative response example. For each of twelve rows exercise schema/model status/code/body/header/correlation positive and negative fixtures; a duplicate key and checksum mismatch fixture must detect incorrect behavior, not merely restate expected values.
+3. Compare 12 operation count, six S&OP/six Capacity, no new endpoint, no removed success/business status, no shared schema change except explicit new response components and `info.version`/annex pointer.
+4. Distinguish these schema/model checks from real HTTP/JWT/Mongo, producer uptake, consumer consent, guard, publication, pack promotion or rollout.
